==================== CLAUDE ANALYSIS, CYCLE 1 (rounds 0-1) ====================
What happened: 2 rounds (13.4 + 16.3 min), 19-unit T1 roster, 12 fresh generated matchups per generation,
seeded from the overnight Pawn-mirror champion. Both rounds promoted a child (c2 then c1).
The in-round scores are NOT comparable (each round benchmarks on new matchups plus new hall-of-fame
opponents), so I re-measured on one identical batch: 60 unseen matchups x 4 seeds x 2 sides x 4 baselines
(1920 battles per brain, scripts/analyze_matchups.py, saved in analysis_cycle1.txt).

  overnight Pawn champion -> after 2 rounds:  win 54% -> 62%, timeout 26% -> 20%, loss 20% -> 19%
  underdog fights 32% -> 45% wins, even 59% -> 66%, overdog 76% -> 78%
  scouts 37% -> 51%, brawl 47% -> 57%, line 56% -> 65%, raid 73% -> 79%, artillery 50% -> 53%
  bots 57% -> 66%, vehicles 40% -> 49%, mixed bot+veh sides 70% -> 74%
  vs hold 72 -> 84%, kite 46 -> 55%, orbit 37 -> 46% (still 52% timeouts), charge 59 -> 61%

What we learned:
 1. The improvement is on matchups the council never trained on, so it is generalising, not memorising.
    The overnight champion was already decent on unfamiliar units (54% overall); most of the gain
    came from underdog, scout and brawl fights, where the Pawn-trained habits were wrong
    (it kited the wrong things and sat out of range).
 2. Vehicles (49%) and artillery (53%) are the weakest groups. Vehicles turn slowly and cannot
    strafe like bots; artillery has a minimum useful engagement geometry (long range, slow reload).
    The council has no inputs for turn rate or reload time, so it cannot tell them apart from bots.
 3. charge is near its ceiling (61%): a third of the matchups are underdog by design, and charging
    opponents trade evenly. Do not read that number as a failure.
 4. orbit still times out half the time. A timeout costs 0.6 for even matchups, so the objective keeps
    pressing on it, but the gain per round there is small.

What to change (applied for cycle 2):
 - Swap in the extended roster (26 units: + Janus, Samson, Mist, Skimmers, Anaconda/Snapper hover
   tanks) so breadth keeps growing instead of the council specialising on 19 units.
 - Raise --min-gain from 0.005 to 0.01: round scores are on different batches, so tiny gains are noise.
 - Keep population/seeds as they are; rounds stay about 15 minutes.
What to watch next: vehicle and artillery win rates, and whether the run starts to overfit the
hall-of-fame opponents (compare the identical batch, not the round score).
Ideas not yet tried: inputs for own turn rate and reload time; a platoon-level focus-fire target.
