==================== CLAUDE ANALYSIS, CYCLE 2 (rounds 2-3) ====================
What happened: extended 26-unit roster, min-gain 0.01. Round 2: no child beat the champion (kept).
Round 3: c3 promoted at +0.019 on that round's benchmark. Round scores swung from +0.007 (round 2) to
+0.242 (round 3) for essentially the same brain, i.e. the benchmark batch (12 fresh matchups) moves the
score far more than any child does.

Identical-batch check (60 unseen matchups x 4 seeds, new roster; analysis_cycle2.txt):
  overnight Pawn champion 60% win | cycle-1 champion (round 1) 62% | current champion (round 3) 62%
=> cycle 2 produced NO measurable improvement; the round-3 promotion is noise (r1 and now are within 1 SE).
The gain over the Pawn champion also shrank on this batch (60 -> 62, versus 54 -> 62 in cycle 1's batch):
the extended roster is easier for the old champion, or most of the available gain was taken in cycle 1.

New yardstick (answers "is the win rate asymptotic?"): metal is a poor measure of strength (24 Fleas
have a lot of metal and almost no power; 4 Rockets cannot hit 19 Farks). I added each fight's POWER ratio
(health x damage/s, the doctrine's own measure) to the matchup names and the analysis. By power:
  stronger (>=1.5x): 87% win, 6% timeout, 7% loss   (vs charge 95, hold 96, kite 80, orbit 76)
  close:             68% win                         (vs charge 67, hold 84, kite 63, orbit 55)
  weaker (<=0.67x):  28% win, 33% timeout, 39% loss  (vs charge 18, hold 78, kite 12, orbit 3 / 92% timeouts)
Reading: against a charging or kiting opponent a weaker side loses for physical reasons, so that part is
close to a ceiling. Real headroom is where we are stronger or even and still fail to finish: stronger vs
kite (17% losses) and orbit (20% timeouts), close vs kite/orbit (63%/55%). That is chasing evasive units,
and it likely depends on speed and turn rate, which the council cannot see.

What to change (applied for cycle 3):
 - Benchmark with 48 matchups and 6 seeds (--eval-matchups 48 --eval-seeds 6) instead of 12 matchups x 16
   seeds, so promotions stop following batch luck.
 - Keep min-gain 0.01, run 4 more rounds (2 cycles of 2 rounds) and judge on the fixed analysis batch.
Not yet changed, candidates if cycle 3 is flat too: add own/enemy speed-advantage and turn-rate inputs so the
council can learn when chasing is hopeless; a platoon focus-fire target; accept the plateau and move on to the
objective stage (attack/defend a target) as planned.
